{"title":"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 13","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~0:40)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~0:40)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Two real numbers are selected independently at random from the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/8/0a843f746fa1baae8bb70d171f959327f2651c2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[-20, 10]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" />. What is the probability that the product of those numbers is greater than zero?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/0/650a3f6aa360beee643bdcc9982188dad3d727a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{1}{9} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{4}{9} \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{5}{9} \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"367\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For the product of two numbers to be greater than zero, they either have to both be negative or both be positive. The interval for a positive number is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of the total interval, and the interval for a negative number is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/7547747b133453d3c209cff6cfbf129aa67a6947.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. Therefore, the probability the product is greater than zero is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/4/a74a71fdd8e6514e00ac335ccd72565ca5c4f101.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} \\times \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{1}{9} + \\frac{4}{9} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)} \\frac{5}{9}}\\]\" width=\"268\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can plot this problem on the coordinate grid. First, plot the possible region [the square with endpoints <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/8/8481878e7b0c2c65920281a130e1514f4d790097.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(10,10), (-20, 10), (-20, -20), (10, -20)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"317\" height=\"20\" />]. Including quadrant 3 and 4 allows for the second number selected to be negative. Then, we can find our possible region which is if both numbers are positive or if both numbers are negative. Lets take the first case in which both numbers are positive: The region which has these desired values is the square [<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/8/2e8b8547ca8c02940c0e66117b3702749afa8ef4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(10,10) ,(0,0) ,(10,0) ,(0,10)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"220\" height=\"18\" />]. Similarly if both numbers are negative we get the square [<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/4/cb485024f90344f670ffc8ff9fbe7802401adb7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-20,-20), (0,0), (-20,0) ,(0,-20)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"277\" height=\"18\" />] Then we find the area of the possible region <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/8/6385796cd8a9b1172979e82b3f3983908d85a609.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30\\cdot 30 = 900$\" width=\"100\" height=\"12\" /> and then find the areas of our positive regions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/b/f3b085668dbec5f7ac7ce468298ca36deca25cf5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10\\cdot 10=100$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"100\" height=\"13\" />  and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/3/eb303f03d490ec19c25a1d4e060a4b9da7b370cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20\\cdot 20 = 400$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"100\" height=\"13\" />. Adding and simplifying we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/7/f179880ceef8a5fe71c980ee12a47fbd58f2b1e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{100+400}{900}=\\frac{5}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"116\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We will use complementary counting. The probability that the product is negative can be found by finding the probability that one number is positive and the other number is negative. The probability of a positive number being selected is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/a/ebafcc60a28d8759185d7808c60ea53efc73938e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />, and the probability of a negative number being selected is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/1/611d08a8c57a214b2b964bba16da8157a5b81c9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac23$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />. So, we see that the probability of the product being negative is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/0/5703f03fd9b4802976366ba9b77e1f29c1ee6b2d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2 \\cdot \\frac23 \\cdot \\frac13 = \\frac49.\\]\" width=\"102\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>We multiply by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> because you can either pick the negative or positive number first. \n</p><p>Thus, the probability of the product being positive is \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/a/f5ada3298fa8a411eb9a20dc36a6760b6584fd35.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1-\\frac49 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }\\frac59}\\]\" width=\"127\" height=\"48\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can simplify the problem down by dividing the boundary numbers by 10, which gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/6/3662cf3e2a293fa4e43147c9e9d4751ca02a09e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[-2,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"52\" height=\"20\" />. Then, it is easier for us to come to Solution 2. \nThe probability the product is greater than zero is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/4/a74a71fdd8e6514e00ac335ccd72565ca5c4f101.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} \\times \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{1}{9} + \\frac{4}{9} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D)} \\frac{5}{9}}\\]\" width=\"268\" height=\"48\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_.28Logical.2C_Good_Editing.2C_.7E0:40.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~0:40)\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~0:40)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~0:40)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/fFP59MjTVVQ\">https://www.youtube.com/shorts/fFP59MjTVVQ</a>\n</p><p>~Ebai\n</p><p>Note: the problem where this is linked is covered 1:30-2:10; however the solution focuses mainly on the concept of addition probability, so the solutions above are more in-depth (the video follows solution 1)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B\">2011 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 12\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 14\">Problem 14</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 15\">15</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 16\">16</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 17\">17</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 18\">18</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 19\">19</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 20\">20</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 21\">21</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 22\">22</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 23\">23</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 24\">24</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25\" title=\"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 25\">25</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_10_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AMC 10 Problems and Solutions\"> All AMC 10 Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017024349\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.035 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.05